## Verdict: passed (with thin-evidence observations)

### 1. Reaction-force semantics are documented correctly
Stage force block: source = "fix tool global vector: force ON the indenter, already a reaction load";
manual: "This fix computes a global scalar energy and a global 3-vector of forces (on the indenter)".
The manual also fixes the magnitude law F(r) = -K(r-R)^2, repulsive, F=0 for r>R. Therefore the
force on the indenter from the atoms is the exact reaction to the repulsive load on mobile atoms:
- normal = mean(f_tool[3]) is the load pushing the sphere away from the substrate (+z since atoms
  sit below the center at z=36.145 with R=12), reported as 43.18 nN = 26.95 eV/Ang at K=10 eV/Ang^2;
- tangential = mean(-f_tool[1]) is the +x resistance, 2.72 nN, and the file is explicit that it is
  signed, not sqrt(Fx^2+Fy^2) — honest, since f_tool[2] (transverse) is discarded, not hidden.

### 2. Internal consistency verified against the executed input and analysis code
- commands listed in measurementSemantics.commandDefinitions reproduce in.grinding verbatim
  ("fix tool mobile indent 10 sphere 17.8 17.8 v_ztip 12 units box", "run 800", "fix contact all print 20 ...").
- grinding.py: tail = forces[len//2:] on 41 samples -> indices 20..40 -> steps 1200..1600 = declared
  forceMeanWindow (21 samples); peakNormal = forces[:,2].max() over 800..1600 = declared fullPassWindow
  (41 samples); meanTemperatureK = data[:,7].mean() over the same pass; conversion 1.602176634 nN per eV/Ang is dimensionally right.
- fixedMaxDisplacementAngstrom = 0 matches fix freeze setforce 0 on the base group; boundary f p f makes
  the x axis non-periodic, and only y is unwrapped in the displacement analysis, so no wrap artifact.
- units box is used, so the sphere x/y/z/R are Angstrom under units metal — geometry mapping is sound.

### 3. Energy/temperature scope statements are correct per the manual
Manual: "The default setting for this fix is fix_modify energy no" and the per-particle interaction
energy is K/3 (r-R)^3. The stage therefore correctly excludes indenter energy from pe and makes no
conservation claim. Temperature is the mean of compute Tmobile over all mobile atoms (bath + dynamic),
mean 330 K vs target 300 K (+10.1%), and the stage explicitly says steady state is not demonstrated.

### 4. Remaining weaknesses (disclosed, not hidden; quantitative validity stays insufficient)
- Speed is 10 Ang/ps = 1000 m/s (motionAudit) — orders of magnitude above experiment; no rate-sensitivity
  claim is attempted, and the stage marks numerical = insufficient.
- Nominal depth (end_z = surface + R - 3 Ang) is measured against the initial surface; real penetration
  and pile-up are not extracted — interpretationLimits states this.
- Single pass, single seed, 0.02 ps output spacing (41 samples over 0.8 ps); peak normal force is
  sampled, so a true transient maximum may be missed.
- Minor: grinding.py names the parameter 'stiffnessEVPerAngstromCubed', but per the manual K is
  force/distance^2 (eV/Ang^2); only a label slip, the executed value 10 is unaffected.
- Sample-limited: no experimental or higher-fidelity cross-check exists (task = cannotCompute is consistent).

### Residual risk
Trustworthy as a documented idealized reaction-load definition and as a reproducible qualitative
trend; NOT trustworthy as a quantitative grinding force, hardness, or contact stress.
